### Media trash — authz boundary (uc.memory.delete-media, cr-0110)
# The unauthenticated boundary of the first-party delete/restore endpoints, runnable against ANY instance — no
# account/scope/membership needed. Like the media blob transport (en.memory.media) and cover.http, the
# authenticated happy-path is NOT reproducible from a bare .http: trashing needs the operator-blind scope-grant
# flow (any member of the scope), and the trash→restore→purge lifecycle needs a seeded member + media. Those,
# plus the delete_revision reconcile, the trashed-list (state=trashed) with deletedBy, non-member 404 and the
# retention purge, are covered at the integration layer (MediaTrashSliceTest) and in the web client's live smoke.
#
# Run: ijhttp --env-file http/media/http-client.env.json --env dev http/media/trash.http

### 1. Trash a media without a bearer token → generic 401 (auth before validation)
DELETE {{host}}/api/scopes/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/media/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

> {%
client.test("delete media requires auth", function () {
    client.assert(response.status === 401, "expected 401, got " + response.status);
    client.assert(response.body.code === "authentication_failed", "expected authentication_failed");
});
%}

### 2. Restore a media without a bearer token → 401
POST {{host}}/api/scopes/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/media/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/restore

> {%
client.test("restore media requires auth", function () {
    client.assert(response.status === 401, "expected 401, got " + response.status);
    client.assert(response.body.code === "authentication_failed", "expected authentication_failed");
});
%}
